 cell lines we see an increase  in labeling for those particular  antibodies indicating an  increase in dna double strand breaks  upon washout we notice at the bottom  here  that there is a dramatic decrease in  those particular levels within the  control cells but that those levels  persist or remain within the rad 54b  deficient cells  certainly showing that those dna double  strand breaks are not being adequately  or properly repaired  in the rad 54v deficient cells and of  course we can quantify that and those  differences  are statistically significant relative  to the corresponding  control cells so with that  we certainly are seeing increases in dna  double strand breaks the evidence  earlier on  suggested that it perhaps was through an  apoptotic mechanism  so we went on to again do quantitative  imaging microscopy  this time looking at cleaved caspase-3  which is basically the point at which  the intrinsic and extrinsic pathways  converge so with respect to cleave  caspase-3 we use the positive control  stores foreign which certainly  increases um cleave cast phase 3 in both  of those cell lines  but really the increases in cleave  caspase-3 were restricted to  those rad 54b deficient cells and were  not really evident within those rad 54b  proficient cells and again we can  quantify that information here  so that really led to the current  working model where we can use sirna  based  silencing or alternatively atgm or 2ma2  which all induce increases in loss  subsequently we also identified lcs1  which is a bonafide sod one inhibitor  identified by hero's pharmacist group  all of these induce increases in ross  that correspond with increases in  persistence of dna double strand breaks  um and ultimately  cause death through an apoptotic  mechanism  now these findings for rad 54b certainly  are suggestive that other members  of a homologous recombination repair  pathway may  also be sensitive synthetically for  targeting of software  so we've certainly begun to investigate  other genes that encode  functions within that pathway and have  shown that sod 1  does indeed have broad spectrum  applicability  so for us that's very exciting and we're  going to continue this work  in sort of the combinatorial approaches  that i'll now show you  so this really leads to the end of this  particular vignette can we enhance the  therapeutic killing that we saw  the use of combinatorial approaches and  this is work done by aaron  and chloe a couple of years ago  so here we're trying to identify  synergistic combinations of drugs  so i didn't show you the data you're  going to have to trust me that rad 54b  is synthetic lethal with bmm673 which is  a part  inhibitor so we're looking at everything  in the context of bmn 673 and changing  those concentrations and the different  drugs for trust too  so just to draw your attention and make  sure you understand the concept drug one  by itself has a particular effect  perhaps certain amount of killing drug  two in this case has an equal amount of  killing such that when you add the two  of them together if it's additive it's  simply  the product uh sorry the the result of  these two  added together if it's antagonistic it's  below that additive effect and if it's  synergistic which from a clinical  perspective is what we really want then  it  has if you will super killing in this  particular case  so shown here are just one example of  each of those three categories  we have bmn 673 with 5fu or  5-fluorouridine which is usually part of  a chemotherapeutic regiment for  colorectal calcium you can see the two  different drug concentrations shown  there  and this axis is basically the  percentage of cells  remaining this is an additive result you  can see bmn 673 with two methoxy ester  dial  has an antagonistic effect so it's not a  combination we would want to pursue  and dmn673 in the context of lcs one  certainly has that synergistic impact or  that sweet spot that we would want to  use  forward now from a clinical perspective  obviously these are the ones that we  want to identify and move forward  through pre-clinical tests  but these combinations are also  important because you would not want to  combine these and give them to patients  because these potentially could  increase tumor aggressiveness and or  growth  so what i'm wanting to show you on the  next couple of slides are some of the  movies that we've generated to look at  these combinatorial approaches  and we're really doing this using that  biotech system that we have which is the  biospa 8 in combination with the  citation 3.  so these experiments are performed in 96  well  round bottom plates cells are seated in  that  and what you can see here initially are  those individual cells and as this movie  plays  you'll see them coalesce together form  that tumor spheroid and begin to grow  over the time course of this imaging  which is roughly two weeks  and so what this really allows us to do  is basically try those various different  combinations to see if they  impact on cellular growth and this  particular 3d spheroid model is  obviously much more akin to what you  might see  in an individual although it's very much  smaller  and it's more representative than the  traditional 2d culture systems that  are used by many groups so this next  movie really begins  at this time point here this is where  that tumor spheroid has  formed and it's at this point where bmn  673 as well as lcs1 has been  supplemented into the media  the media is also supplemented with  propidium iodide which is the  fluorescent marker of  death and so as we watch this particular  movie what you'll see  is those cells begin to die in that  tumor spheroid  beginning from the outside and working  their way in towards the core of this  particular sphere  and of course by doing replicates and  multi wells for all of these various  conditions we can basically use that  fluorescence  indicator as a indicator of cell death  and so here we can actually quantify  that we have three different conditions  bmm-673 by itself  lcs one by itself and the two in  combination  you can see the concentration of bmn 673  that we're using really has no effect  on death in these particular tumor  spheroids very low concentration  however lcs one does appear to induce  death as a result but when we combine  those two drugs in those identical  combinations we see this purple line  which is indicative of greater levels  of killing so that synergistic effect  so for us this is very exciting work  that is leading us to additional  pre-clinical models to identify those  best combinatorial approaches to migrate  forward  so as far as an overall summary of  today's talk is concerned  you hopefully can see how we're  interested in sin and its relationship  with cancer  how we develop those quantitative  imaging microscopy approaches  and applied them to identify the  prevalence of sin in cancer to identify  those pathogenic origins or syn genes  and how we can exploit that information  to develop  therapeutic strategies that selectively  exploit cancer cell fibrin defects in  those  thin genes as both single agents as well  as through combinatorial approaches  and finally i'd like to acknowledge  those individuals and groups responsible  for this work  certainly like to highlight all past and  present lab members  as well as a lot of fruitful  collaborations in particular  like to highlight brad larson who was  instrumental in assisting us with our  micronucleus formation assays and  certainly we'll be doing a number of  presentations later today as well  i'd also like to highlight and thank  cancercure manitoba the tumor bank in  particular  and all those women who provided their  bio specimens for us  i'd also like to thank all the funding  groups that are shown along that bottom  row and finally a special thank you to  biotech  for being a wonderful partner that's not  only assisted  us to advance our research but for their  invitation to speak  to you today so with that i thank you  very much  and would be happy to answer any  questions  thank you dr mcmanus for that  informative presentation  we will now start the q a portion of  this webinar and if you have  any questions you'd like to ask please  do so now  just simply click on the ask a question  box  located on the far left of your screen  so let's get started we have quite a few  questions  already coming in  dr mcmanus our first question is can you  describe  the micronuclei assay in more detail  how is this uh quantitative can it be  fully automated  uh so thank you for the question uh yes  i'll start with for the  the end of that question yes it can and  it is actually fully automated so  you essentially collect your images we  generally collect between  um 9 and 16 images per well in a 96 well  plate and the analysis  is basically done by hitting a button  and getting the number of micronuclei  out at the end  so just a little bit more information on  the micronucleus assay itself  how it generally is done is in an  automated fashion  it first and foremost creates a mask  around the nucleus the primary nucleus  itself  we then set a threshold for the distance  from the nucleus  which is basically used as sort of a  surrogate indicator as to the cell body  and so that's a secondary mask and then  we ask  um the program what sort of bodies or  nuclear stain bodies are found  outside the primary mass but within that  sort of cell boundary mass and it can  give us the number of micronuclei  quantify the number of nuclei so we can  create that as as sort of an overall  percentage and we can see  changes in those frequencies depending  on the various different conditions  and statistically compare those with  relevant controls  thank you so much and is it surprising  that we see  amplification of skp1  in some cancers for example ccrcc  and the stomach so  as you would have seen in in some of our  slides  we certainly were looking at skip one  and its relationship to diminished gene  expression  um and that's what we were particularly  interested in with a specific  uh focus on um single  allelic loss heterozygous loss or sort  of those shallow deletions  it's not necessarily surprising in a  variety of different cancer types that  you see a variety of different things  happen  for any given gene the question is to  discern between those driver events and  those passenger events  so as predicted if you take your  favorite gene you certainly will see  instances where it can be amplified or  instances where it can be lost  it's really important to do those  functional analysis to discern whether  or not those gains or losses are those  critical aspects  we by no means suggest that skip one  loss of skip one expression  is the only way that we could get on  some of those particular phenotypes  and in fact with some of the genes that  we're studying independent of skip one  we can show and we're aware that these  genes can be diminished in expression or  increased in expression and both  contribute to those  phenotypes and could be pathogenic  events  thank you so much and we have time for  one more question  what is the role of chromosome alignment  if and if any in the pathways that  you've been describing here  so as far as chromosomal alignment i'm  assuming what you mean is actually  congressional errors to the metaphase  plate during mitosis or alternatively  um lagging chromosomes as chromosomes  segregate  so we certainly can do live cell imaging  microscopy and quantify those there's  certainly  individuals that do a lot of lineage  tracing  we don't tend to do a lot of that with  our particular genes that we're  interested in at the moment unless we  see evidence for there could be  congressional segregation errors  in those we can also capture those  events independent of live cell  imaging basically in traditional  endpoint fixed  if we survey enough cells across 